Elisabeth Riba asks: 

>1) Does it matter which ring goes on which hand?  
  Normally, no.  Rings work the same on either hand.  However, if you
wield a cursed single-handed weapon, you will not be able to put on/
take off rings on your right hand.  So if you've put on a ring of
teleport control on your left hand, and wield a cursed athame, you'll
be unable to put on another ring, even tho you've got a free ring
finger.

>2) How can you fight a floating eye without being paralysed when you
>   don't have a cream pie to smash him with or a blindfold for
>   yourself?

Main answer: Throw stuff at it.  Start with arrows, rock, knives.
  Additional possibilies: Zap it with wand of invisibility, then pound
it.  Wear a shield or amulet of reflection (you'll be imune to its
gaze).  Let your pet take care of it.  Zap it with any ray (except
sleep).  Show it its own reflection in a mirror and ignore it (still
can't hit it, but it will quit following you.)  Make it somebody
else's problem with a ring of conflict.
  This is only a partial list; there are possiblities like taming it
and polymorphing it which are not actually strategies that you'll
often use.

>3) Do the different noises coming from sinks & fountains mean
>   anything?

Clanking pipes, music, etc are meaningless.  Sounds (and messages) you
hear when dropping rings in sink indicate the ring type.


>4) What is this Elbereth thing that everyone talks about?

Others will no doubt supply the whole Elbereth legend, but briefly:
When you engrave Elbereth on the ground (successfully), monsters will
not attack you.  Best results from a wand of fire (uses 1 charge) or
athame (unlimited engravings).  But beware, humans will ignore it, and
monsters can still throw projectiles at you.  And your movement can
erase letters, spoiling the magic.
  I recommend playing in discovery mode and wishing for things, then
engraving with them until you get the hang of it.

>5) I read the way to detect what kinds of wands you have without using them up,
>   and to determine ring types by dropping them in the sink--but how do
>   you discover what kinds of potions or scrolls you have without using
>   identify scrolls?

Wands, scrolls, potions, rings and gems can be more-or-less identified
by the prices shopkeepers will pay/charge for them.  [the "cost"
figure in the mega-spoiler sheet you got.]  The "cost" that sheet
lists is a base price - you generally receive half of that amount when
selling to the shop.  You may have to pay a premium over the base
price, but it is a constant factor (which depends on, at least, your
alignment.)   So, (looking at that sheet) if you sell a potion to a
shop and receive 150 zorkmids, there are only 3 potions with a base
price of 300 - gain level, paralysis and gain ability, and what you
just sold is one of these.  Additionally, only a potion of sickness
will form a coating on darts (etc) when they are #dipped in an unknown
potion [which is a way of eliminating one of the most nasty
possibilities before swigging away].   And water is alway clear.
  This price structure is useful even if you're not buying.  So when
in a shop, if you pick up a green potion and are told it costs 150,
you can #name green potions to green-150 which will serve as a partial
ID when you, say, find one lying around later.

ADDTIONAL, BONUS SPOILER ON IDENTIFICATION!!!

Uncursed scrolls of identify sometimes identify more than one item;
blessed scrolls of identify sometimes identify everything until they
run out.  So you may want to try and maximize your identification.  
  When you read the scroll, it will ask you what types of things you
want identified.  If you just hit <return> it will go thru your pack
in order, asking what you want identified.  To maximize your
identifications, you need to get the things in your pack in their
optimum order.  
  Do this by setting the nofixinv option.  Then if you have, say, 1
amulet, 2 rings, 4 scroll, 5 potions and 2 gems which you'd like to
have identified, work as follows.  Drop the amulet, pick it up.  It's
now alphabetically the last item in your inventory.  Now drop the
rings, and pick them up.  Then the scrolls, then the potions, then the
gems.  You'll usually want to not carry any food so that you can
maximize your other pickup, also only carry one of each unidentied
thing.
  You may want to set the nosortpack option in order to better see the
order which you've imposed.
  Now read the scroll of identify.  Hit <return> to the initial
question, and "n" to each item until you get to the amulet.  Then
reply "a"; if you're moderately lucky it will ID the amulet and the
rings; if you're hot, it will ID everything.
  You will probably want to set your options back to sortpack,fixinv
after this.
